      "text": "To find the radius of the circle, we need to determine the distance from the center of the circle to one of the given points, as the radius is the same for any point on the circle.\n\nGiven points are \\((0, 4)\\) and \\((1, 3)\\). The center of the circle is on the \\(x\\)-axis, so its coordinates can be written as \\((a, 0)\\).\n\nThe distance from the center \\((a, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\) is the radius \\(r\\). We can use the distance formula to express this:\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(a - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{a^2 + 16}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the distance from the center \\((a, 0)\\) to the point \\((1, 3)\\):\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(a - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(a - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions represent the radius \\(r\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{a^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(a - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSquaring both sides to eliminate the square roots, we get:\n\\[\na^2 + 16 = (a - 1)^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side:\n\\[\na^2 + 16 = a^2 - 2a + 1 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the equation:\n\\[\na^2 + 16 = a^2 - 2a + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(a^2\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n16 = -2a + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 10 from both sides:\n\\[\n6 = -2a\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by -2:\n\\[\na = -3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the center, we substitute \\(a = -3\\) back into the expression for the radius:\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
