      "text": "To solve for the radius of the circle, we need to determine the equation of the circle and then find the radius using the center and a point on the circle. Since the center of the circle lies on the x-axis, its coordinates can be written as \\((h, 0)\\). The general equation of a circle with center \\((h, k)\\) and radius \\(r\\) is:\n\n\\[\n(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\n\\]\n\nSince the circle passes through the points \\((0, 4)\\) and \\((1, 3)\\), these points must satisfy the circle's equation. Substituting \\((0, 4)\\) into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n(0 - h)^2 + (4 - 0)^2 = r^2 \\implies h^2 + 16 = r^2 \\implies h^2 + 16 = r^2\n\\]\n\nNext, substituting \\((1, 3)\\) into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n(1 - h)^2 + (3 - 0)^2 = r^2 \\implies (1 - h)^2 + 9 = r^2 \\implies (1 - h)^2 + 9 = r^2\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to \\(r^2\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (1 - h)^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right side, we get:\n\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = 1 - 2h + h^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we find:\n\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(h^2\\) from both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n6 = -2h \\implies h = -3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(h = -3\\), we can substitute this value back into the equation \\(h^2 + 16 = r^2\\) to find \\(r^2\\):\n\n\\[\n(-3)^2 + 16 = r^2 \\implies 9 + 16 = r^2 \\implies 25 = r^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius \\(r\\) is:\n\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nThe radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
