      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the radius of the circle, we need to determine the distance from the center of the circle to either of the given points of tangency. Since the center of the circle lies on the \\(x\\)-axis, its coordinates can be expressed as \\((h, 0)\\). The distance from this center to either point \\((0,4)\\) or \\((1,3)\\) is the radius of the circle.\n\nThe distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((0,4)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{h^2 + 16}\n\\]\nThe distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((1,3)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\nSince both distances are equal to the radius \\(r\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\nSquare both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (h - 1)^2 + 9\n\\]\nExpand the right side:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 1 + 9\n\\]\nSimplify the equation by subtracting \\(h^2\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\nRearrange to solve for \\(h\\):\n\\[\n16 - 10 = -2h \\implies 6 = -2h \\implies h = -3\n\\]\nNow that we have \\(h = -3\\), we can find the radius \\(r\\) by calculating the distance from the center \\((-3, 0)\\) to either \\((0,4)\\) or \\((1,3)\\):\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(-3 - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
